WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.367 --> 00:00:02.868
-Our next guest is
a Grammy nominated comedian

00:00:02.868 --> 00:00:04.604
who's latest special
"Hello, World"

00:00:04.604 --> 00:00:06.439
is streaming now on Prime Video.

00:00:06.439 --> 00:00:07.674
Let's take a look.

00:00:07.674 --> 00:00:09.242
-So, one part
of your brain is smart

00:00:09.242 --> 00:00:10.576
and the other part is dumb.

00:00:10.576 --> 00:00:11.977
You can trick your own brain.

00:00:11.977 --> 00:00:14.380
That's how dumb
the dumb part is.

00:00:14.380 --> 00:00:15.614
[ Laughter ]

00:00:15.614 --> 00:00:17.283
It's in the same head.

00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:18.751
[ Laughter ]

00:00:18.751 --> 00:00:19.919
That's embarrassing.

00:00:19.919 --> 00:00:22.355
It's like, you don't
hear this going on, dude?

00:00:22.355 --> 00:00:25.157
If you're in a bad mood,
they tell you to fake smile

00:00:25.157 --> 00:00:27.260
and your dumb part
will think it's a good day.

00:00:27.260 --> 00:00:29.428
[ Laughter ]

00:00:29.428 --> 00:00:32.131
I mean, you don't hear
the planning going on?

00:00:32.131 --> 00:00:34.434
Like, you're a part
of the planning.

00:00:34.434 --> 00:00:38.404
-Please welcome to the show
the very funny Nate Bargatze.

00:00:38.404 --> 00:00:40.406
[ Cheers and applause ]

00:00:40.406 --> 00:00:54.186 line:0%
♪♪

00:00:54.186 --> 00:00:55.187
Welcome, Nate!

00:00:55.187 --> 00:00:56.222
-Hey. Thank you, man.

00:00:56.222 --> 00:00:57.289
-I've been a fan
for a long time.

00:00:57.289 --> 00:00:58.491
I'm so happy you're here.

00:00:58.491 --> 00:00:59.726
-I'm excited to be here, dude.

00:00:59.726 --> 00:01:01.026
This is fun.

00:01:01.026 --> 00:01:03.128
-You're from Tennessee,
you live in Tennessee,

00:01:03.128 --> 00:01:05.365
but you you spent a fair amount
of time here in New York City.

00:01:05.365 --> 00:01:08.301
-Yeah, I lived here for
eight and a half years,

00:01:08.301 --> 00:01:11.171
like, 2004 to '12.

00:01:11.171 --> 00:01:13.239
-And what were your first
impressions of the city?

00:01:13.239 --> 00:01:14.941
-It was great. It's a lot, Dude.

00:01:14.941 --> 00:01:17.677
I mean, it's such a different
thing coming from Tennessee,

00:01:17.677 --> 00:01:19.011
like, haven't left.

00:01:19.011 --> 00:01:21.947
I remember first seeing when
people parallel parked,

00:01:21.947 --> 00:01:24.450
like, they bump cars, you know?

00:01:24.450 --> 00:01:28.787
And me, I saw it and I was like,
I'm watching a hit and run.

00:01:28.787 --> 00:01:33.526
I watched -- I wrote their
license plate down.

00:01:33.526 --> 00:01:38.497
I was gonna call the NYPD,
who's probably the most buys.

00:01:38.497 --> 00:01:42.235
And just be like, "You won't
believe what's going on."

00:01:42.235 --> 00:01:44.403
And, like, I mean, I thought
it was like a giant thing,

00:01:44.403 --> 00:01:46.338
and then you just learn
that's what --

00:01:46.338 --> 00:01:48.408
that's what everybody does.
That's what bumpers are for.

00:01:48.408 --> 00:01:49.742
-Yeah, that's right.
-Yeah.

00:01:49.742 --> 00:01:51.043
-That's -- We were like,
"Nope that's what they're for."

00:01:51.043 --> 00:01:52.979
-I actually became
unbelievable at it,

00:01:52.979 --> 00:01:57.249
and I remember I one time parked
and mine was touching two cars.

00:01:57.249 --> 00:01:58.551
So I got real good.

00:01:58.551 --> 00:02:00.620
I though, it fits --
If we can do it, I will do it.

00:02:00.620 --> 00:02:01.754
-Yeah, exactly.
-Yeah.

00:02:01.754 --> 00:02:03.256
-If there's a skill
out there to be learned,

00:02:03.256 --> 00:02:04.357
you might as well learn it.

00:02:04.357 --> 00:02:06.459
Now, I'm surprised
that you had this skill.

00:02:06.459 --> 00:02:08.428
A lot of people
I think know this now,

00:02:08.428 --> 00:02:10.563
but when you're an early
comedian in the city,

00:02:10.563 --> 00:02:12.131
one of the things you do
to get stage time

00:02:12.131 --> 00:02:14.133
is you go out and you
sort of are a comedy barker,

00:02:14.133 --> 00:02:16.869
you try to get people
to go to shows.

00:02:16.869 --> 00:02:18.404
And sometimes if you're walking
around, certainly, like,

00:02:18.404 --> 00:02:20.806
Times Square, you see really
enthusiastic young comedians

00:02:20.806 --> 00:02:21.807
trying to convince people.

00:02:21.807 --> 00:02:22.842
You did that.

00:02:22.842 --> 00:02:24.911
You don't quite have
that barker vibe.

00:02:24.911 --> 00:02:26.713
-Yeah, it's this energy...
-Yeah.

00:02:26.713 --> 00:02:28.647
-...that's just like,
"Hey, you wanna come --"

00:02:28.647 --> 00:02:29.816
I wouldn't even do that.

00:02:29.816 --> 00:02:31.117
"You wanna, like,
come to this comedy show?

00:02:31.117 --> 00:02:32.151
And people were like, "No."

00:02:32.151 --> 00:02:34.821
Like, they would
grab the flyer --

00:02:34.821 --> 00:02:37.289
It was at Boston Comedy Club,
it was called New York,

00:02:37.289 --> 00:02:40.626
and they would get the flyer
and just drop it

00:02:40.626 --> 00:02:42.027
in front of me.

00:02:42.027 --> 00:02:44.197
Like ju-- It was almost like
they just wanted to litter.

00:02:44.197 --> 00:02:46.365
Like, they could have
just said no.

00:02:46.365 --> 00:02:49.201
But they were like, they just
wanted me to pick it up.

00:02:49.201 --> 00:02:50.603
-They wanted to
save other people

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:52.238
from having to be
asked this question.

00:02:52.238 --> 00:02:53.672
-They grabbed them all
and they threw them.

00:02:53.672 --> 00:02:56.542
They go, "Just go.
Move back home."

00:02:56.542 --> 00:02:58.811
-This last special is wonderful.

00:02:58.811 --> 00:02:59.879
We were talking backstage.

00:02:59.879 --> 00:03:01.113
I think we have
very similar wives.

00:03:01.113 --> 00:03:03.816
Your wife seems to know
how everything works,

00:03:03.816 --> 00:03:06.418
very helpful around the house.

00:03:06.418 --> 00:03:08.053
I don't know anything.

00:03:08.053 --> 00:03:09.889
You make a great show
of the fact that

00:03:09.889 --> 00:03:11.290
you don't know anything.

00:03:11.290 --> 00:03:14.059
And a boiler guy showed up
at you house.

00:03:14.059 --> 00:03:15.895
and I've had this happen
before where they assume

00:03:15.895 --> 00:03:17.630
that you're the guy
with the information.

00:03:17.630 --> 00:03:21.301
-Yeah, we say "water heater."
-Okay.

00:03:21.301 --> 00:03:24.671
-So, boiler, I didn't even know
what you're talking about.

00:03:24.671 --> 00:03:28.708
So, I mean,
I don't know what's happening.

00:03:28.708 --> 00:03:31.911
But we, yeah, in the special,

00:03:31.911 --> 00:03:34.046
I talk about how our
water heater broke

00:03:34.046 --> 00:03:36.315
and the guy who came over
was asking me where it was.

00:03:36.315 --> 00:03:38.618
And I don't know where --
Like, I was like, I don't know.

00:03:38.618 --> 00:03:42.054
And -- 'Cause it's like, I would
just walk by at the wrong time.

00:03:42.054 --> 00:03:45.424
A lot of times when --
'Cause she has people over
there,

00:03:45.424 --> 00:03:47.226
just coming over
and doing stuff.

00:03:47.226 --> 00:03:48.894
She's always fixing something.

00:03:48.894 --> 00:03:51.063
And they talk to me.

00:03:51.063 --> 00:03:52.732
And, like, I keep,
like, looking.

00:03:52.732 --> 00:03:54.534
I'm like -- And she answers.

00:03:54.534 --> 00:03:55.734
But they keep looking at me.

00:03:55.734 --> 00:03:57.103
I'm like, what are you
not getting, dude?

00:03:57.103 --> 00:04:00.706
Like, I don't know
these answers.

00:04:00.706 --> 00:04:02.207
We have a new roof.

00:04:02.207 --> 00:04:04.110
And I found out
from my neighbor.

00:04:04.110 --> 00:04:05.678
A whole new roof.

00:04:05.678 --> 00:04:07.714
[ Laughter ]

00:04:09.382 --> 00:04:10.916
I was, like, out in the
driveway, he goes,

00:04:10.916 --> 00:04:11.917
"How's the new roof?"

00:04:11.917 --> 00:04:13.219
And I go, "What's that?"

00:04:13.219 --> 00:04:17.089
And then she went and got
a new roof when I was gone,

00:04:17.089 --> 00:04:19.492
and I didn't even --

00:04:19.492 --> 00:04:20.726
There was no, like, heads up.

00:04:20.726 --> 00:04:22.495
Like, I'm not in --
Like, it didn't matter.

00:04:22.495 --> 00:04:24.363
It's not like I'm gonna
live differently.

00:04:24.363 --> 00:04:26.031
[ Laughter ]

00:04:26.031 --> 00:04:29.101
But I was --
It's embarrassing, you know,

00:04:29.101 --> 00:04:30.936
to be sitting there
and be in my 40's.

00:04:30.936 --> 00:04:33.406
-I also, I mean, it seems like
you're the same way as me.

00:04:33.406 --> 00:04:35.841
Like, I would assume if my wife
decides we need a new roof,

00:04:35.841 --> 00:04:38.077
I agree, right?

00:04:38.077 --> 00:04:39.412
-Yeah, oh yeah.
-I would never put my foot down

00:04:39.412 --> 00:04:42.181
and be like,
"There -- Not on my --"

00:04:42.181 --> 00:04:43.982
"This roof is the roof
we're gonna have."

00:04:43.982 --> 00:04:45.618
-No, I would -- Yeah.

00:04:45.618 --> 00:04:47.687
I stay out, and I would like to.

00:04:47.687 --> 00:04:49.421
I would like to do that,
to go, like,

00:04:49.421 --> 00:04:52.991
"Over my dead body,
are we gonna get a new roof."

00:04:52.991 --> 00:04:54.560
I love this roof so much.

00:04:54.560 --> 00:04:55.728
[ Laughter ]

00:04:55.728 --> 00:04:57.897
I don't even know how to tell
if a roof's bad.

00:04:57.897 --> 00:04:59.098
Like, I don't --

00:04:59.098 --> 00:05:00.233
[ Laughter ]

00:05:00.233 --> 00:05:02.435
Like, I mean, if it's just gone,

00:05:02.435 --> 00:05:04.236
you know?

00:05:04.236 --> 00:05:06.972
-If water poured in,
I would say we need a new roof.

00:05:06.972 --> 00:05:08.241
But other than that...

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:10.309
-If she told me we don't,
I would say okay.

00:05:10.309 --> 00:05:12.011
Like, I would be like,
"Well, she knows."

00:05:12.011 --> 00:05:13.045
-Yeah.
-You know?

00:05:13.045 --> 00:05:14.046
-She's like,
"Oh, this is a bucket.

00:05:14.046 --> 00:05:15.347
You just get
a bucket for this."

00:05:15.347 --> 00:05:17.283
-You're like, "All right."
-"Where's the buckets?"

00:05:17.283 --> 00:05:19.685
That's where? I don't know.

00:05:19.685 --> 00:05:21.521
[ Laughter ]

00:05:21.521 --> 00:05:24.457
-You -- But this is --
You used to read,

00:05:24.457 --> 00:05:26.091
so I'm surprised you don't know
about a water heater,

00:05:26.091 --> 00:05:28.360
'cause you used to do --
You were a meter reader. Yes?

00:05:28.360 --> 00:05:29.428
-A water meter reader.

00:05:29.428 --> 00:05:30.963
-Water meter reader.
-Yes.

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:34.200
And, so, when I first started,
like, this is the job I had.

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.868
This is the job I'll go back to
when all this falls apart.

00:05:35.868 --> 00:05:37.136
-Okay.

00:05:37.136 --> 00:05:38.204
This is a Tennessee job?

00:05:38.204 --> 00:05:40.038
-This is a Tennessee,
Wilson County,

00:05:40.038 --> 00:05:42.674
we read water meters, so,
like, you know, to see, like,

00:05:42.674 --> 00:05:45.277
how much water you used,
and you get billed for it.

00:05:45.277 --> 00:05:48.514
And, so, it was in 2001
and it was when 9/11 happened.

00:05:48.514 --> 00:05:51.217
So right after 9/11 happened,

00:05:51.217 --> 00:05:54.354
they got worried that they were
gonna come after our water

00:05:54.354 --> 00:05:56.689
at Wilson County --
the terrorists.

00:05:56.689 --> 00:05:57.690
And...

00:05:57.690 --> 00:05:59.726
[ Laughter ]

00:05:59.726 --> 00:06:02.194
So, we --

00:06:02.194 --> 00:06:03.362
This is all we --

00:06:03.362 --> 00:06:05.297
For one year,
we would take shifts

00:06:05.297 --> 00:06:07.499
and you would guard
the water towers.

00:06:07.499 --> 00:06:12.004
And we spent the night just
begging the Taliban to try.

00:06:12.004 --> 00:06:14.373
[ Laughter ]

00:06:14.373 --> 00:06:15.808
Just --

00:06:15.808 --> 00:06:18.677
Come get in Mount Juliet's
water, dude.

00:06:18.677 --> 00:06:21.314
You gonna have the nerve

00:06:21.314 --> 00:06:23.983
to poison Wilson County's water?

00:06:23.983 --> 00:06:25.818
Not on our watch.

00:06:25.818 --> 00:06:28.454
We were out there all night
in the woods waiting for them.

00:06:28.454 --> 00:06:29.455
-Yeah.
-Yeah.

00:06:29.455 --> 00:06:30.823
-Well, it was effective.

00:06:30.823 --> 00:06:32.758
They didn't --
Word got back to them.

00:06:32.758 --> 00:06:33.759
They didn't come near it.

00:06:33.759 --> 00:06:34.927
-Ah, water's been great.

00:06:34.927 --> 00:06:36.228
-Yep.
-It's been great.

00:06:36.228 --> 00:06:40.433
-You, uh, you do tell a lot
of jokes about your wife.

00:06:40.433 --> 00:06:43.068
I should note,
she comes out always...

00:06:43.068 --> 00:06:44.837
I mean, you tell 'em.
You know.

00:06:44.837 --> 00:06:46.138
She comes out
a lot smarter than you.

00:06:46.138 --> 00:06:47.640
-Oh, yeah, yeah.
-Makes it helpful,

00:06:47.640 --> 00:06:49.475
but do you run everything
by her ahead of time?

00:06:49.475 --> 00:06:52.044
-Yeah, she knows everything.

00:06:52.044 --> 00:06:53.345
Like, I tell her --

00:06:53.345 --> 00:06:54.647
Like, she knows all the jokes
that I'm gonna say.

00:06:54.647 --> 00:06:56.115
Like, I don't want to surpri--

00:06:56.115 --> 00:06:57.249
Like, you know,
say anything mean, or, like,

00:06:57.249 --> 00:06:58.750
you know, stuff like that.

00:06:58.750 --> 00:07:01.521
So she knows all the jokes, and
she's got a sense of humor of,

00:07:01.521 --> 00:07:03.623
like, she likes making fun, too.

00:07:03.623 --> 00:07:08.261
So it works that I can say the
stuff and I let her hear it.

00:07:08.261 --> 00:07:10.496
I may have one joke --
I don't have one,

00:07:10.496 --> 00:07:13.232
but it's like, if one was great,
though, it would be tough.

00:07:13.232 --> 00:07:16.668
Like, 'cause it's like, how hard
it is to get a good joke.

00:07:16.668 --> 00:07:18.237
If she's like,
"I don't like that one,"

00:07:18.237 --> 00:07:20.539
I was like, "Ah, it gets
a pretty big applause, so..."

00:07:20.539 --> 00:07:21.808
Like, I don't know.

00:07:21.808 --> 00:07:23.242
-"I mean, I did get you
a new roof."

00:07:23.242 --> 00:07:24.944
-I'd go -- Yeah!

00:07:24.944 --> 00:07:26.645
I'd go, "We'll get another one!
We'll get two roofs!"

00:07:26.645 --> 00:07:28.014
-Yeah, you want one?
-Might as well put another one

00:07:28.014 --> 00:07:29.515
on top of it. Double it up."

00:07:29.515 --> 00:07:30.549
-Double the roof.
-Why aren't more people

00:07:30.549 --> 00:07:31.650
not doing more roofs?

00:07:31.650 --> 00:07:32.985
You should do two roofs.

00:07:32.985 --> 00:07:34.820
-You have a daughter.
-Yep.

00:07:34.820 --> 00:07:36.923
-Does -- Who gets
the laughs at home?

00:07:36.923 --> 00:07:38.524
Does your --
Is your daughter funny?

00:07:38.524 --> 00:07:39.558
Does she think you're funny?

00:07:39.558 --> 00:07:40.826
-I don't think she --

00:07:40.826 --> 00:07:44.396
I mean, she gets that
people think I'm funny,

00:07:44.396 --> 00:07:46.031
but she'd very funny.

00:07:46.031 --> 00:07:50.336
And she -- And I like it 'cause
she'll get my wife going,

00:07:50.336 --> 00:07:52.571
and then once my wife starts
laughing, like, our daughter,

00:07:52.571 --> 00:07:54.440
she just doesn't let up.
It's like a show.

00:07:54.440 --> 00:07:56.909
So, it's, like, very fun to see,
like she just gets it,

00:07:56.909 --> 00:07:59.545
and she's like, all right, I got
her, and it just keeps rolling.

00:07:59.545 --> 00:08:02.347
She does, like, she does voices,

00:08:02.347 --> 00:08:04.083
like very more
talented than I am.

00:08:04.083 --> 00:08:05.484
Like, I'm just this.

00:08:05.484 --> 00:08:07.520
[ Laughter ]

00:08:07.520 --> 00:08:08.988
-Well --
-She can -- all over the place.

00:08:08.988 --> 00:08:11.256
-Well, next generations
sometimes are.

00:08:11.256 --> 00:08:12.458
It's a 2.0 situation.

00:08:12.458 --> 00:08:15.761
I remain a very big fan
of what you do on stage.

00:08:15.761 --> 00:08:16.895
Thank you so much
for being here, man.

00:08:16.895 --> 00:08:18.097
What a pleasure to meet you.
-Absolutely.

00:08:18.097 --> 00:08:20.032
-You guys, that's Nate Bargatze.

00:08:20.032 --> 00:08:22.101
"Hello World" is streaming now
on Prime Video.

00:08:22.101 --> 00:08:23.870
We'll be right back
with more "Late Night"

